7.5 Piping with dplyr
Above, we used what’s called “normal” grammar, but the strengths of dplyr lie in combining several functions using pipes.
Pipes take the input on the left side of the |&gt; symbol and pass it in as the first argument to the function on the right side. This is why dplyr data manipulation functions take .data as their first argument!
coffee_beans |&gt; 
  harvest() |&gt; 
  roast() |&gt; 
  grind() |&gt; 
  add("hot water") |&gt; 
  filter() |&gt; 
  drink()
raw_data |&gt;
  convert_units() |&gt;
  calculate_outcome_metric() |&gt;
  fit_model() |&gt;
  plot_result()
Note: Older versions of R didn’t allow |&gt; so you might see %&gt;% instead. This pipe operator requires the R package {magrittr} to use. They are (nearly) equivalent!
Since the pipe grammar is unlike anything we’ve seen in R before, let’s repeat what we’ve done above using pipes:

filter for the month of June
select Day, Temp


airquality |&gt;
  filter(Month == 6) |&gt;
  select(Day, Temp)
#&gt; # A tibble: 30 × 2
#&gt;      Day  Temp
#&gt;    &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;
#&gt;  1     1    78
#&gt;  2     2    74
#&gt;  3     3    67
#&gt;  4     4    84
#&gt;  5     5    85
#&gt;  6     6    79
#&gt;  7     7    82
#&gt;  8     8    87
#&gt;  9     9    90
#&gt; 10    10    87
#&gt; # ℹ 20 more rows

First we summon the airquality data frame and pass it on to the next step using the pipe symbol |&gt;
The second steps is the filter() function. Then, we take that output and send it to select.
In this case we don’t specify which data object we use in the call to select() or to filter() since we’ve piped it in.
Aside: There is a good chance you have encountered pipes before in the shell/terminal. In R, a pipe symbol is |&gt; while in the shell it is |. But the concept is the same!






Tip



You can make pipes using Command+Shift+M instead of typing the characters individually. You might find this quicker and more ergonomic!

12.4 ggplot2 and tidy data
ggplot2 plays nice with dplyr and pipes. If you want to manipulate your data specifically for one plot but not save the new dataset, you can call your dplyr chain and pipe it directly into a ggplot call.

# This combines the subsetting and plotting into one step
medal_counts |&gt;
  filter(Medal == "Gold") |&gt;
  filter(NOC == "USA") |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = Year, y = count)) + 
  geom_line()

14.4 Facets
Sometimes you might have too much data to easily display on one plot, and you want to split it into multiple similar plots. facet_wrap (easy, usually right) and facet_grid (easier to control with multiple variables) help with that!

airquality_long &lt;- airquality |&gt;
  mutate(day_since_start = row_number()) |&gt;
  select(-c(Month, Day)) |&gt;
  tidyr::pivot_longer(
    -day_since_start, 
    names_to = "parameter", 
    values_to = "obs"
  ) 

airquality_long |&gt;
  ggplot() +
  aes(x = day_since_start, y = obs, color = parameter) +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~parameter, scales = "free")

16.3 Writing functions

16.3.1 The Basic Syntax
Create a function:

# variable_name &lt;- function(arg1, arg2) {
  # code goes here!
#}

add_two &lt;- function(x){
  x + 2
}

add_two(4)
#&gt; [1] 6

A function takes 0 or more arguments, and will return the last unassigned line of code, as above, or you can more explicitly return() a value.

add_two &lt;- function(x){
  y &lt;- x + 2
  return(y)
}

add_two(4)
#&gt; [1] 6

add_two &lt;- function(x){
  return(x + 2)
}

add_two(4)
#&gt; [1] 6

See how R changes the color of function and return? These are “special” words.


16.3.2 A more complicated example
Let’s say that body mass index (BMI) is something you often have to calculate. You could remember the formula and type it out each time: bmi = weight/height**2
But it might be less error prone to write a function!

calculate_bmi &lt;- function(height_in_cm, weight_in_kg){
  # Fill in the function code here!
}

Lets try it out!

library(dplyr)

starwars |&gt;
  select(name, height, mass) |&gt;
  mutate(bmi = calculate_bmi(height_in_cm = height, weight_in_kg = mass))
#&gt; # A tibble: 87 × 3
#&gt;    name               height  mass
#&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;               &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt;  1 Luke Skywalker        172    77
#&gt;  2 C-3PO                 167    75
#&gt;  3 R2-D2                  96    32
#&gt;  4 Darth Vader           202   136
#&gt;  5 Leia Organa           150    49
#&gt;  6 Owen Lars             178   120
#&gt;  7 Beru Whitesun Lars    165    75
#&gt;  8 R5-D4                  97    32
#&gt;  9 Biggs Darklighter     183    84
#&gt; 10 Obi-Wan Kenobi        182    77
#&gt; # ℹ 77 more rows

17.1 if-else syntax
We have used logical expressions before:
Often you want to use logical expressions to control what code is evaluated.
For this, we will use if and else!
Here’s a simple example to illustrate the syntax.
val &lt;- rnorm(1)
if (val &lt; 0) {
  print("val is negative")
} else {
  print("val is positive")
}
#&gt; [1] "val is negative"
Note how RStudio colors these words differently? They are special key words.
We can chain together if statements using else if.
val &lt;- rnorm(1, mean = 0, sd = 1)
val
#&gt; [1] 0.5591984
if (val &lt; -1) { 
  print("val is more than one standard deviation below the mean.")
} else if (abs(val) &lt;= 1) {
  print("val is within one standard deviation of the mean.")
} else {
  print("val is more than one standard deviation above the mean.")
}
#&gt; [1] "val is within one standard deviation of the mean."
In general, the { brackets are only needed if you have multiple R expressions, but R will complain when an else starts a line of code, so generally using the { is good practice. That said, this works just fine:
if (val &lt; 0) print("val is negative") else print("val is positive")
#&gt; [1] "val is positive"

# better:
if (val &lt; 0) {
  print("val is negative") 
} else {
  print("val is positive")
}
#&gt; [1] "val is positive"

17.2 The condition in an if statement
There are two rules for the if () statement:

The value evaluated must be TRUE or FALSE

NA, NULL, and NaN will all produce (different) errors

The value evaluated must be of length 1

If more than one value is provided, depending on your user settings, either the first value will be evaluated or you will encounter an error
If less than one value (c() is of length 0), you will encounter an error


Zero evaluates to FALSE, all other numbers evaluate to TRUE. In general strings are not converted to booleans.

# These will both produce errors
vec &lt;- c(4, 8)
vec &lt; 6
#&gt; [1]  TRUE FALSE
if (vec &lt; 6) {
  print("vec is less than 6")
} else {
  print("vec is not less than 6")
}
#&gt; Error in `if (vec &lt; 6) ...`:
#&gt; ! the condition has length &gt; 1

vec &lt;- NA
if (vec &lt; 6) {
  print("vec is less than 6")
} else {
  print("vec is not less than 6")
}
#&gt; Error in `if (vec &lt; 6) ...`:
#&gt; ! missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed

If you anticipate missing data, a more robust alternative is to use isTRUE():

isTRUE(NA)
#&gt; [1] FALSE
isTRUE(NULL)
#&gt; [1] FALSE
isTRUE(1)
#&gt; [1] FALSE
isTRUE(FALSE)
#&gt; [1] FALSE
isTRUE(TRUE)
#&gt; [1] TRUE
isTRUE(as.logical(1))
#&gt; [1] TRUE

vec &lt;- NA
if (isTRUE(vec &lt; 6)) {
  print("vec is less than 6")
} else {
  print("vec is not less than 6")
}
#&gt; [1] "vec is not less than 6"

If you want to be able to handle vectors, all or any are helpful:

# `any` checks for at least one TRUE
vec &lt;- c(4, 8)
if (any(vec &lt; 6)) {
  print("at least one value is less than 6")
} else {
  print("no values are less than 6")
}
#&gt; [1] "at least one value is less than 6"

# `all` requires every value to be TRUE
vec &lt;- c(4, 8)
if (all(vec &lt; 6)) {
  print("all values are less than 6")
} else {
  print("at least one value is not less than 6")
}
#&gt; [1] "at least one value is not less than 6"

21.2 Breakout 4b

We previously saw a function for calculating BMI, which looked something like this:


calculate_bmi &lt;- function(height, weight){ # assume height in m, weight in kg
  weight / height ** 2
}

calculate_bmi(1.70, 70)
#&gt; [1] 24.22145

Modify the function so that the user supplies, as a string, the unit for height, accepting either c(“cm”, “m”, or “in”). Then, if necessary, convert the height to the appropriate unit before calculating BMI.
Use the following conversion factors: 1m = 100 cm = 39.37 inches

# write your solution here
calculate_bmi &lt;- function(height, weight, unit = "m"){ 
  if (unit == "cm") {
    height &lt;- height / 100
  } else if (unit == "in") {
    height &lt;- height / 39.37
  } 
  weight / height ** 2
}

# if it is correct, the following should return TRUE
identical(calculate_bmi(1.70, 70, "m"), calculate_bmi(170, 70, "cm"))
#&gt; [1] TRUE
identical(
  round(calculate_bmi(1.70, 70, "m"), 3), 
  round(calculate_bmi(1.7*39.37, 70, "in"), 3)
)
#&gt; [1] TRUE

21.4 Breakout 4d
In the following examples, try to predict what value f(1) will return without running the code.

What is f(1)?

Three: g(x) returns 2, so g(x) + 1 returns 3

g &lt;- function(x) {
  x + 1
}

f &lt;- function(x) {
  g(x) + 1
}

f(1)
#&gt; [1] 3


What will the following return?


f(1)
f(1, 3)
f(1) will use the default argument of y defined in f(x), and so g(x, y) evaluates to g(1, 2) which is 3.
f(1, 3) uses the supplied arguments for both x and y, so evaluates to 1 + 3 or 4


g &lt;- function(x, y = 1) {
  x + y
}

f &lt;- function(x, y = 2) {
  g(x, y) 
}

f(1)
#&gt; [1] 3
f(1, 3)
#&gt; [1] 4


What is f(1)?

Here, y is not supplied to g, and so the default value of 1 is used, even though y is defined in the global environment.
The answer is therefore 2.

g &lt;- function(x, y = 1) {
  x + y
}
y &lt;- 1000
f &lt;- function(x) {
  g(x) 
}

f(1)
#&gt; [1] 2

